`_index_to_einsum_variable()` is only called by `_build_proj_equation()` and `_build_attention_equation()`. In this test, the first layer call triggers `build()` because the layer is newly created. `build()` calls `_build_proj_equation(query_rank - 1, 1, 2)`, `_build_proj_equation(key_rank - 1, 1, 2)`, and `_build_proj_equation(value_rank - 1, 1, 2)`; with rank-3 inputs, each has `free_dims=2, bound_dims=1, output_dims=2`, so each makes `2+1+2 = 5` calls, totaling 15. Then `_make_output_dense()` calls `_build_proj_equation(query_rank - 1, 2, 1)`, which is `2+2+1 = 5` more. Also during build, `_build_attention(output_rank)` calls `_build_attention_equation(rank=4, attn_axes=(1,))`; that function calls `_index_to_einsum_variable` 4 times to build `target_notation`, plus once more for the non-batch/non-last axis in `source_notation`, totaling 5. The later `call()` path uses the built equations and does not call `_index_to_einsum_variable` again. So the total is `15 + 5 + 5 = 25`.

## Answer
{"call_count":25}
